---
// MEGT › Before & after (DESIGN.md › Layout › MEGT case study, item 6): section heading, then
// three comparisons, each a Before card beside an After card (white cards on bg-subtle, as the
// info cards). 120px under The product; comparisons 80 apart.
import SectionHeading from './SectionHeading.astro';
import Collage from './Collage.astro';
import FinderWindow from './FinderWindow.astro';
import SharingDialog from './SharingDialog.astro';
import interventionRule from '../../assets/images/megt/41_section-group-before-and-after-canvas-behavior__image-form-builder-intervention-rule.png';
import spreadsheet from '../../assets/images/megt/42_section-group-before-and-after-canvas-behavior__image-legacy-spreadsheet-logic.png';
import workflowBuilder from '../../assets/images/megt/43_section-group-before-and-after-canvas-behavior__image-visual-workflow-builder.png';
import addRole from '../../assets/images/megt/44_section-group-before-and-after-canvas-behavior__image-add-new-role.png';

// After visuals: the screenshot inset 14 on a pale blue frame (artwork colour).
const AFTER_BG = '#E6EDFB';
const BEFORE_BG = '#E9EAEE';
---

<section id="before-and-after" class="before-after" aria-labelledby="before-after-title">
  <SectionHeading
    id="before-after-title"
    eyebrow="Before & after"
    lead="The workshop with MEGT surfaced plenty of pain points, but three kept coming back. Each one ended up shaping a different part of the tool."
    width={687}
  >
    What the tool replaced, and what it became.
  </SectionHeading>

  <div class="comparisons">
    <div class="comparison">
      <h3 class="text-h6">Assessment creation was copy-paste every time</h3>
      <ul class="cards">
        <li class="card">
          <FinderWindow />
          <div class="copy">
            <h4 class="text-title-l">Before</h4>
            <p class="text-body-m">Every new assessment was pieced together by hand, with questions copied from spreadsheets or old forms into Google Forms or Typeform. Nothing was versioned, so once a template changed, there was no telling which copies were out of date.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <Collage
            width={556}
            height={325}
            bg={AFTER_BG}
            label="The form builder's intervention rules, with a question library panel and a three-panel editing layout."
            layers={[{ src: interventionRule, box: [14, 14, 528, 297], crop: [0.05, -0.05, 100, 104.34], radius: 8, shadow: true }]}
          />
          <div class="copy">
            <h4 class="text-title-l">After</h4>
            <p class="text-body-m">Questions now live in one library and get reused, not rebuilt. The client wanted the finished assessment to show one question at a time, but building it that way would have meant clicking through page after page. So the editing canvas shows the whole form at once, and only the published version goes one question per page.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="comparison">
      <h3 class="text-h6">Conditional logic lived only in people's heads</h3>
      <ul class="cards">
        <li class="card">
          <Collage
            width={556}
            height={325}
            bg={BEFORE_BG}
            label="A spreadsheet of logic formulas, with a broken #REF! cell."
            layers={[{ src: spreadsheet, box: [-1, -17, 558, 359] }]}
          />
          <div class="copy">
            <h4 class="text-title-l">Before</h4>
            <p class="text-body-m">The logic behind each assessment sat in spreadsheets next to the form. It was easy to break, and anyone new needed a full walkthrough, which quickly became overwhelming.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <Collage
            width={556}
            height={325}
            bg={AFTER_BG}
            label="The visual workflow builder: question nodes connected on a canvas, with the logic settings on the right."
            layers={[{ src: workflowBuilder, box: [14, 14, 528, 297], radius: 8, shadow: true }]}
          />
          <div class="copy">
            <h4 class="text-title-l">After</h4>
            <p class="text-body-m">The logic now lives inside the form itself, as nodes anyone can follow. A node canvas was new ground for the team, though I’d designed something similar before for an AI voice platform. We studied how Typeform and other node-based tools handle it, and kept every connection as simple as we could for people who aren’t technical.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="comparison">
      <h3 class="text-h6">No access control meant no accountability</h3>
      <ul class="cards">
        <li class="card">
          <SharingDialog />
          <div class="copy">
            <h4 class="text-title-l">Before</h4>
            <p class="text-body-m">Everything sat in shared Excel files, so anyone with access could see and change all of it, with no structure for who was responsible for what.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <Collage
            width={556}
            height={325}
            bg={AFTER_BG}
            label="The Add new role screen, with permissions grouped by area and a toggle for each."
            layers={[{ src: addRole, box: [14, 14, 528, 297], radius: 8, shadow: true }]}
          />
          <div class="copy">
            <h4 class="text-title-l">After</h4>
            <p class="text-body-m">Every person now sees only what their role allows. For permissions, we modelled the setup on how Discord handles roles, and it came out simpler than any permission screen I’d designed before. Real user feedback will be its final test.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .before-after {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-48);
    margin-top: var(--space-120);
  }

  .comparisons {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-80);
    container-type: inline-size;
  }

  .comparison {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-32);
  }

  h3,
  h4 {
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }

  .cards {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-24);
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .card {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-16);
    padding: var(--space-16);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    background-color: var(--bg-cards);
  }

  .copy {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-12);
  }

  .copy p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  /* Too narrow for Before and After side by side: stack them. */
  @container (max-width: 700px) {
    .cards {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    /* Mobile: 48 between sections (user's decision). */
    .before-after {
      margin-top: var(--space-48);
      gap: var(--space-32); /* Mobile: content 32 below the heading block. */
    }

    /* Mobile: 36 between comparisons (user's decision). */
    .comparisons {
      gap: var(--space-36);
    }

    /* Mobile: title 24 above its cards, cards 16 apart (user's decision). */
    .comparison {
      gap: var(--space-24);
    }

    .cards {
      gap: var(--space-16);
    }

    /* Mobile: Before / After in title-m, a step below the 16px comparison title (user's decision). */
    h4 {
      font-size: var(--title-m-size);
      line-height: var(--title-m-line);
    }
  }
</style>
